.TH QUADRF-APPLY-HOSTNAME 8 "August 2026" "QuadRF" "QuadRF"
.SH NAME
quadrf-apply-hostname \- set or lock the QuadRF mDNS name
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B quadrf-apply-hostname
.B rename
.I name
.br
.B quadrf-apply-hostname
.B set
.I name
.br
.B quadrf-apply-hostname
.B reset
.br
.B quadrf-apply-hostname
.B lock
.BR yes | no
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rename
writes
.B QUADRF_HOSTNAME
and runs
.BR quadrf apply
so avahi, nginx, the local server certificate and dnsmasq follow
.IR name .
The Linux hostname is not changed; it remains
.BR quadrf .
.BR quadrf-mdns-alias
calls this when another unit already holds the current name, unless
.B QUADRF_HOSTNAME_LOCK
is yes.
.PP
.B set
is the operator path: same apply as
.B rename
but the lock does not block it. The Network Setup page uses this when
the hostname field is saved. A name other than
.B quadrf
turns the lock on; reset turns it off.
.PP
.B reset
sets the name back to
.B quadrf
and applies. If another QuadRF still owns
.IR quadrf.local
and the lock is off, this unit will take
.IR quadrf-2.local
again.
.PP
.B lock
only writes
.BR QUADRF_HOSTNAME_LOCK .
It does not apply. Lock on stops automatic rename; the operator can still
.BR set
or
.BR reset .
.PP
The web control panel invokes set, lock and reset through the sudoers rule in
.IR /etc/sudoers.d/quadrf .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR quadrf (1),
.BR quadrf.conf (5)
